"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 25. The surgical findings included a Gleason 4+3, pT3a stage, positive surgical margins, and absence of seminal vesicle invasion and lymph node metastasis. The biopsy demonstrated a higher grade (Gleason 4+4) than the resection specimen (Gleason 4+3). The absence of lymph node metastasis is a favorable finding.\n\nCountervailing factors include the positive surgical margins and the higher grade identified on biopsy compared to the resection. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data significantly widens the uncertainty regarding the trajectory of recurrence. The current horizon suggests a time frame for recurrence, but this is a heuristic range. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and subsequent trajectory would be crucial in refining this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 27.61 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 22.09–33.13 months; its coverage has not been validated."
